---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const learn = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/git-integration';
---

<Base
  title="Fabric Git integration | Jeremy Hoover"
  description="How I helped design the Git integration experience in Microsoft Fabric: the source control panel, status model, conflict resolution, selective branching, and code comparison."
>
  <article class="section case-study case-study--blue">
    <header class="case-hero">
      <div class="container container--narrow">
        <a href={`${base}/#projects`} class="back"><span class="back-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Back to selected projects</span></a>
        <span class="tag"><span>Product design</span><span>Microsoft Fabric</span><span>Git integration</span></span>
        <h1>Making Git feel native inside a data platform</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Fabric workspaces are shared, live environments. Git is built around private branches,
          explicit commits, and merges. I worked with interaction design to bring those two
          models together: the source control panel, the status system, conflict resolution,
          selective branching, and code comparison.
        </p>
        <div class="outcomes-strip" aria-label="Project outcomes">
          <div class="outcome">
            <span class="outcome__num">6</span>
            <span class="outcome__label">design decisions shipped in public Fabric UI</span>
          </div>
          <div class="outcome">
            <span class="outcome__num">2-way</span>
            <span class="outcome__label">sync model between workspace and Git</span>
          </div>
          <div class="outcome">
            <span class="outcome__num">Public</span>
            <span class="outcome__label">Microsoft Learn docs and Fabric blog announcements</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="case-body">
      <div class="container case-shell">
        <section class="case-summary" aria-labelledby="summary-title">
          <div class="reading-column">
            <h2 id="summary-title">At a glance</h2>
            <dl class="case-meta">
              <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Content and product design, in partnership with interaction design</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Contribution</dt><dd>Screens and mockups, information architecture, status and action language, and overall flow decisions</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Collaboration</dt><dd>Interaction designers, product managers, and engineering</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Users</dt><dd>Data engineers, BI developers, and analytics teams adopting software development practices</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Shipped. Branched workspaces, selective branching, and code comparison released in preview.</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Evidence</dt><dd>Public Microsoft Learn documentation and Fabric blog posts. No confidential material.</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </section>

        <nav class="case-toc" aria-label="Fabric Git case study sections">
          <span class="case-toc__label">Jump to a section</span>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="#problem">Problem</a></li>
            <li><a href="#constraints">Constraints</a></li>
            <li><a href="#direction">Panel structure</a></li>
            <li><a href="#status">Status model</a></li>
            <li><a href="#conflicts">Conflicts</a></li>
            <li><a href="#branching">Branching</a></li>
            <li><a href="#compare">Compare</a></li>
            <li><a href="#reflection">Reflection</a></li>
          </ul>
        </nav>

        <section id="problem" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>The problem</h2>
          <p>
            A Fabric workspace is a shared runtime. When someone edits a notebook, report, or
            pipeline there, everyone sees the change immediately. Software teams protect shared
            code with branches, commits, and reviews, but data teams in Fabric had no native way
            to work like that.
          </p>
          <p>
            Git integration connects a workspace to a branch in Azure DevOps or GitHub. The hard
            part was not the connection. It was helping people who had never used Git understand
            what state their work was in, which direction a change was moving, and what would
            happen to their work before they acted.
          </p>
          <p class="todo"><strong>Jeremy:</strong> add one concrete signal you saw, such as a support theme, research finding, or a common user mistake. Keep it public-safe.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="constraints" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Users and constraints</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Two audiences.</strong> Experienced developers expected Git conventions. BI authors and analysts often met Git for the first time here.</li>
            <li><strong>Shared workspaces.</strong> One workspace connects to one branch, so an action by one person affects everyone in it.</li>
            <li><strong>Items, not files.</strong> People think in reports, semantic models, and notebooks, not in folders of definition files.</li>
            <li><strong>Destructive operations.</strong> Updating a workspace or accepting incoming changes can overwrite work. Every irreversible action needed to say so plainly.</li>
            <li><strong>Consistency.</strong> The experience had to match deployment pipelines and the rest of Fabric's CI/CD tools.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="direction" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Decision 1: Organize the panel by direction</h2>
          <p>
            Git has many verbs: commit, push, pull, fetch, and merge. Instead of exposing those
            verbs, the source control panel answers one question first: which way is this change
            going? <strong>Changes</strong> lists what is moving from the workspace to Git.
            <strong>Updates</strong> lists what is moving from Git into the workspace. Each tab
            has one primary action, Commit or Update all.
          </p>
          <p class="todo"><strong>Jeremy:</strong> what other structure was considered (for example, one combined list, or Git verbs as tabs), and why did direction win?</p>
        </section>

        <figure class="evidence-figure" aria-labelledby="panel-caption">
          <div class="mock-panel" role="img" aria-label="Simplified source control panel with a Changes tab showing two items and a commit box, and an Updates tab">
            <div class="mock-panel__header">
              <span>Source control</span>
              <span class="mock-badge" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
            </div>
            <div class="mock-panel__tabs">
              <span class="is-active">Changes <small>Workspace → Git</small></span>
              <span>Updates <small>Git → workspace</small></span>
            </div>
            <ul class="mock-panel__list">
              <li><span class="status status--modified">M</span>Sales report<em>Modified</em></li>
              <li><span class="status status--new">+</span>Customer notebook<em>New</em></li>
            </ul>
            <div class="mock-panel__footer">
              <span class="mock-input">Describe your changes (optional)</span>
              <span class="mock-button">Commit</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <figcaption id="panel-caption">
            <strong>One direction per tab, one primary action per direction.</strong>
            A simplified reconstruction. See the shipped panel in
            <a href={`${learn}/git-get-started`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Get started with Git integration on Microsoft Learn ↗</a>.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section id="status" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Decision 2: A small, honest status vocabulary</h2>
          <p>
            Every item in a connected workspace shows its Git status, and the source control icon
            shows a count of uncommitted changes. The set is deliberately small: New, Modified,
            Deleted, Conflict, and Same change in the panel, and Uncommitted or Synced on the
            item. Each status pairs an icon with a word, so meaning never depends on color alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            "Same change" covers a case Git users rarely think about: the item changed in both
            places, but in the same way. Naming it separately keeps it out of the conflict flow,
            so people don't stop to resolve a problem that doesn't exist.
          </p>
          <p class="todo"><strong>Jeremy:</strong> which status names were debated? For example, was "Synced" chosen over "Up to date" or "Committed"? What drove it?</p>
        </section>

        <figure class="evidence-figure" aria-labelledby="status-caption">
          <dl class="status-grid">
            <div><dt><span class="status status--new">+</span>New</dt><dd>Exists in the workspace, not yet in Git</dd></div>
            <div><dt><span class="status status--modified">M</span>Modified</dt><dd>Changed in the workspace since the last sync</dd></div>
            <div><dt><span class="status status--deleted">−</span>Deleted</dt><dd>Removed from the workspace, still in Git</dd></div>
            <div><dt><span class="status status--conflict">!</span>Conflict</dt><dd>Changed differently in both places</dd></div>
            <div><dt><span class="status status--same">=</span>Same change</dt><dd>Changed in both places, identically</dd></div>
            <div><dt><span class="status status--synced">✓</span>Synced</dt><dd>Workspace and branch match</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <figcaption id="status-caption">
            <strong>The status model.</strong> Each state answers "do I need to act?" in a word.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section id="conflicts" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Decision 3: Name conflict choices by outcome</h2>
          <p>
            When the same item changes in the workspace and in Git, Commit is disabled and the
            panel offers <strong>Resolve conflicts</strong>. For each item, people choose
            between outcomes, not Git mechanics:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Accept incoming changes</strong> replaces the workspace version with the Git version. Workspace changes are lost.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep current content</strong> keeps the workspace version. The item stays uncommitted until it is committed.</li>
            <li><strong>Compare and commit changes</strong> opens a side-by-side view before deciding.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The third option matters most for new Git users. It gives people a safe step when
            they aren't sure, instead of forcing an irreversible choice.
          </p>
          <p class="todo"><strong>Jeremy:</strong> what were the earlier labels (for example "Overwrite" or "Discard"), and what made you push for outcome-based wording?</p>
        </section>

        <figure class="evidence-figure" aria-labelledby="conflict-caption">
          <div class="choice-grid" role="img" aria-label="Three conflict resolution choices with their outcomes">
            <div class="choice"><strong>Accept incoming changes</strong><span>Workspace becomes the Git version</span><em class="risk">Workspace edits are lost</em></div>
            <div class="choice"><strong>Keep current content</strong><span>Workspace version stays</span><em>Commit it later to finish</em></div>
            <div class="choice choice--safe"><strong>Compare and commit changes</strong><span>Review both versions side by side</span><em>Decide with evidence</em></div>
          </div>
          <figcaption id="conflict-caption">
            <strong>Choices framed as outcomes, with consequences stated up front.</strong>
            See the shipped flow in
            <a href={`${learn}/conflict-resolution`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Conflict resolution on Microsoft Learn ↗</a>.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section id="branching" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Decisions 4 and 5: Branch only what you need, and show where it came from</h2>
          <p>
            Branching out originally copied every item in a workspace. For large workspaces that
            was slow, and it increased the risk of unintended changes. <strong>Selective
            branching</strong> lets people pick specific items and automatically adds the items
            they depend on, so the new workspace still works.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Branched workspaces</strong> make the relationship visible. A feature
            workspace now shows which workspace it came from in navigation and in source
            control, so people don't lose track of where their work belongs.
          </p>
          <p class="todo"><strong>Jeremy:</strong> how did you handle auto-included items in the UI? Were they shown, locked, or explained? What was the alternative?</p>
        </section>

        <figure class="evidence-figure" aria-labelledby="flow-caption">
          <ol class="agent-flow">
            <li><span class="agent-flow__step">1</span><strong>Shared workspace</strong><span>Connected to the main branch</span></li>
            <li><span class="agent-flow__step">2</span><strong>Selective branch</strong><span>Pick items, dependencies added</span></li>
            <li><span class="agent-flow__step">3</span><strong>Branched workspace</strong><span>Linked back to its source</span></li>
            <li><span class="agent-flow__step">4</span><strong>Compare and commit</strong><span>Review before it leaves</span></li>
            <li><span class="agent-flow__step">5</span><strong>Pull request</strong><span>Merge in the Git provider, then update</span></li>
          </ol>
          <figcaption id="flow-caption">
            <strong>The end-to-end developer loop</strong>, as described in the
            <a href="https://community.fabric.microsoft.com/t5/Fabric-Updates-Blog/Introducing-new-Git-developer-experiences-in-Microsoft-Fabric/ba-p/5172181" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fabric announcement ↗</a>.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section id="compare" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Decision 6: One compare pattern across CI/CD</h2>
          <p>
            Compare code changes adds a diff view in three places: before committing, before
            updating from Git, and while resolving a conflict. It intentionally matches the
            comparison people already know from other Git tools and from Fabric deployment
            pipelines, so one learned pattern works across the CI/CD experience.
          </p>
          <p class="todo"><strong>Jeremy:</strong> what was your part in keeping Git and deployment pipelines consistent? Any specific labels or entry points you aligned?</p>
        </section>

        <section id="outcome" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>What shipped</h2>
          <p>
            The source control panel, status model, and conflict resolution flow are part of
            Fabric Git integration. Branched workspaces, selective branching, and code comparison
            shipped in preview. All of it is documented publicly on Microsoft Learn.
          </p>
          <p class="todo"><strong>Jeremy:</strong> add any public outcome you can cite, such as community reactions, a conference demo, or a follow-up feature that built on this.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="reflection" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>
          <p>
            The main design challenge was translation. Git's model is precise, but its
            vocabulary assumes a developer audience. Each decision here moved the experience
            from Git mechanics toward outcomes: which way a change is going, whether you need to
            act, and what you will lose if you continue.
          </p>
          <p>
            Working as a content designer alongside interaction design, I learned that structure
            and language decisions are the same decision in a product like this. The tab names
            are the information architecture.
          </p>
        </section>

        <aside class="inspect-work" aria-labelledby="inspect-title">
          <div>
            <span class="tag">Public and inspectable</span>
            <h2 id="inspect-title">See the shipped experience</h2>
            <p>Microsoft Learn documents every screen described here, and the Fabric blog explains the newest branching and compare features.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="inspect-work__actions">
            <a href={`${learn}/git-get-started`} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn">Git integration docs ↗</a>
            <a href="https://community.fabric.microsoft.com/t5/Fabric-Updates-Blog/Introducing-new-Git-developer-experiences-in-Microsoft-Fabric/ba-p/5172181" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn--outline">Read the announcement ↗</a>
          </div>
        </aside>

        <hr class="divider" />
        <nav class="case-pagination" aria-label="Case study navigation">
          <a href={`${base}/work/statecraft-israel`} class="case-pagination__link">
            <span class="case-pagination__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="case-pagination__content"><span class="case-pagination__direction">Previous project</span><span class="case-pagination__title">Statecraft: Israel</span></span>
          </a>
          <a href={`${base}/work/ai-design-repo`} class="case-pagination__link case-pagination__link--next">
            <span class="case-pagination__content"><span class="case-pagination__direction">Next project</span><span class="case-pagination__title">AI design repository</span></span>
            <span class="case-pagination__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
<style>
  .case-study { padding-top: 0; }
  .tag { display: inline-block; margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  h1 { margin-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-6); }
  .lede { max-width: 58ch; margin-bottom: var(--space-12); font-size: var(--size-xl); line-height: 1.6; }
  .outcome { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-1); transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .outcome:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .outcome__num { font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 13cqw, 2rem); line-height: 1.1; white-space: nowrap; }
  .outcome__label { max-width: 24ch; font-size: var(--size-sm); color: var(--muted); }
  .case-shell { max-width: calc(var(--narrow) + 6rem); }
  .reading-column { width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin-inline: auto; }
  .content-section { scroll-margin-top: 90px; }
  .content-section h2, .case-summary h2 { margin-bottom: var(--space-6); }
  .content-section:not(:first-child) h2 { margin-top: var(--space-16); }
  .content-section p { margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .content-section ul { margin: var(--space-4) 0 var(--space-6) var(--space-6); }
  .content-section li { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
  code { padding: 0.15em 0.4em; border-radius: 3px; background: var(--portfolio-blue-soft); color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-family: var(--f-mono); font-size: 0.875em; }
  .case-meta { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-3); }
  .case-meta div { padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-left: 4px solid var(--portfolio-blue-soft); border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .case-meta div:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .case-meta dt { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .case-meta dd { color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  .case-toc { max-width: var(--narrow); margin: var(--space-8) auto var(--space-16); padding-block: var(--space-6); border-block: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .case-toc__label { display: block; margin-bottom: var(--space-3); color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.1em; line-height: 1.2; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .case-toc ul { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem 0; margin: 0; list-style: none; }
  .case-toc li { --toc-divider: var(--portfolio-coral); display: flex; align-items: center; }
  .case-toc li + li { margin-left: var(--space-4); }
  .case-toc li + li::before { content: ''; width: 2px; height: 16px; margin-right: var(--space-4); border-radius: 999px; background: var(--toc-divider); }
  .case-toc li:nth-child(3n) { --toc-divider: var(--portfolio-yellow); }
  .case-toc li:nth-child(3n + 1) { --toc-divider: var(--portfolio-blue); }
  .case-toc a { display: inline-flex; min-height: 32px; align-items: center; color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 650; line-height: 1.3; text-decoration: none; }
  .case-toc a:hover { color: var(--portfolio-blue); text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-color: var(--portfolio-coral); text-decoration-thickness: 2px; text-underline-offset: 5px; }
  .evidence-figure { width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin: var(--space-12) auto var(--space-16); }
  .evidence-figure figcaption { width: 100%; max-width: none; margin-top: var(--space-4); color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); }
  .evidence-figure figcaption strong { color: var(--portfolio-ink); }
  .architecture { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-3); padding: var(--space-4); border: 1px solid #b8c9ee; border-radius: 20px; background: var(--portfolio-blue-soft); }
  .architecture__item { position: relative; min-height: 210px; padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid rgba(16,26,45,0.1); border-radius: 14px; background: #fff; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .architecture__item:hover { z-index: 2; transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .architecture__item::after { content: '→'; position: absolute; top: 50%; right: -1.05rem; z-index: 1; display: grid; width: 1.55rem; height: 1.55rem; place-items: center; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--portfolio-blue); color: #fff; font-weight: 800; transform: translateY(-50%); }
  .architecture__item:last-child::after { content: none; }
  .architecture__number { display: block; margin-bottom: var(--space-8); color: var(--portfolio-coral); font-family: var(--f-heading); font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.08em; }
  .architecture h3 { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); color: var(--portfolio-blue); }
  .architecture p { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.875rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  .agent-flow { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(5, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-2); margin: 0; padding: var(--space-4); border: 1px solid #e1c35f; border-radius: 20px; background: #fff8da; list-style: none; }
  .agent-flow li { position: relative; display: flex; min-height: 170px; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-2); padding: var(--space-4); border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; color: var(--muted); transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .agent-flow li:hover { z-index: 2; transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .agent-flow li::after { content: '→'; position: absolute; top: 50%; right: -0.75rem; z-index: 1; color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 1.25rem; font-weight: 800; transform: translateY(-50%); }
  .agent-flow li:last-child::after { content: none; }
  .agent-flow__step { display: grid; width: 1.75rem; height: 1.75rem; place-items: center; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--portfolio-blue); color: #fff; font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 800; }
  .agent-flow strong { color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .agent-flow li > span:last-child { font-size: 0.8rem; line-height: 1.45; }
  .proof-list { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); gap: var(--space-3); margin: var(--space-8) auto 0; }
  .proof-list a { display: flex; min-height: 176px; flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; gap: var(--space-3); padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 14px; background: #fff; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .proof-list a:hover { text-decoration: none; transform: translateY(-4px); box-shadow: 0 12px 28px rgba(16,26,45,0.12); }
  .proof-list span { color: var(--portfolio-coral); font-size: 0.7rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.07em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .proof-list strong { color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.35; }
  .proof-list small { margin-top: auto; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.8rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  .response-quote { width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin: var(--space-8) auto; }
  .response-quote blockquote { margin: 0; padding: var(--space-8); border-left: 5px solid var(--portfolio-coral); border-radius: 0 16px 16px 0; background: #fff; color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-family: var(--f-heading); font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 2.5vw, 1.5rem); font-style: normal; line-height: 1.5; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .response-quote blockquote:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .response-quote figcaption { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.1rem; padding: var(--space-3) 0 0 var(--space-8); color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); }
  .response-quote figcaption strong { color: var(--portfolio-ink); }
  .inspect-work { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.4fr) minmax(220px, 0.6fr); width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); gap: var(--space-8); align-items: center; margin: var(--space-16) auto 0; padding: clamp(1.5rem, 5vw, 3rem); border-radius: 20px; background: var(--portfolio-blue); }
  .inspect-work .tag { color: var(--portfolio-yellow); }
  .inspect-work h2 { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); color: #fff; }
  .inspect-work p { color: rgba(255,255,255,0.82); }
  .inspect-work__actions { display: grid; gap: var(--space-3); }
  .inspect-work .btn { display: flex; min-height: 48px; align-items: center; justify-content: center; background: var(--portfolio-coral); color: #fff; text-align: center; }
